	<annotations>
		<text id="0" type="text/markdown" rect="(-60, 260, 300, 70)" font-family="Helvetica" font-size="14">**Machine Problem 2**  
Classification and Clustering</text>
		<text id="1" type="text/markdown" rect="(-60, 650, 250, 90)" font-family="Helvetica" font-size="14">**Step 1: Load the data**  
Breast Cancer Wisconsin, 683 samples, 9 features, target = type.</text>
		<text id="2" type="text/markdown" rect="(260, -110, 330, 90)" font-family="Helvetica" font-size="14">**Step 2: k-Means**  
Two clusters. The pivot table counts benign and malign samples in each cluster.</text>
		<text id="3" type="text/markdown" rect="(440, 430, 300, 110)" font-family="Helvetica" font-size="14">**Step 2: Hierarchical clustering**  
Distances first, then the tree is cut into the top 2 clusters.</text>
		<text id="4" type="text/markdown" rect="(1020, 100, 250, 90)" font-family="Helvetica" font-size="14">**Step 2: Pivot table**  
rows = type, columns = Cluster, count.</text>
		<text id="5" type="text/markdown" rect="(1020, 260, 250, 90)" font-family="Helvetica" font-size="14">**Step 3: Box plot**  
Clump thickness for each cluster.</text>
		<text id="6" type="text/markdown" rect="(1020, 420, 260, 110)" font-family="Helvetica" font-size="14">**Step 4: Scatter plot**  
Clump thickness vs Unif_Cell_Size, color = type, shape = Cluster.</text>
		<text id="7" type="text/markdown" rect="(160, 1150, 320, 110)" font-family="Helvetica" font-size="14">**Step 5: Split the data**  
80% training sample and 20% test sample, each shown in a table.</text>
		<text id="8" type="text/markdown" rect="(660, 560, 340, 90)" font-family="Helvetica" font-size="14">**Step 6: Models**  
Six models that predict type, tuned for the highest CA with 10-fold cross-validation.</text>
		<text id="9" type="text/markdown" rect="(1160, 1090, 280, 130)" font-family="Helvetica" font-size="14">**Steps 6-7: Test and Score**  
Cross-validation on the training sample, then Test on test data with the 20% test sample.</text>
		<arrow id="10" start="(60, 650)" end="(60, 615)" fill="#C1272D" />
